A||||2||MCordray||Creaking Brakes Eliminated||||6
Z||000000||MCordray||10-11-1999||06:46 PM||||I think I may have solved the brake rattle & creaking on my '86 GT. I replaced the Autozone pads with Carbomet pads and now both the creaking and rattling are gone! I thoroughly cleaned the rotors, calipers, mounting bolts, and mounting bolt holes with brake cleaner. I also used anti-seize lubricant on the caliper bolts and mounting holes. I did not use any type of anti-squeal because I think that may contribute to the brake rattle (back of pad loosens from the  caliper pistion). So, whether it's the Carbomets or just the cleaning and anti-seize, I'm not sure. But I can finally go down the street without waking the neighbors (no creaking, rattling, or squealing).||||reg||6
Z||000001||theogre||10-11-1999||08:50 PM||||Could easily been crappy pads.... Wouldn't be the first time that's happened.<P>In addition to lubing the slide bolts and holes, I also lube the "reaction stops". These are the areas on the caliper bracket that the caliper contacts durring braking. I use anti sieze for this since it's hard to wash off. ((the stops are the part of the bracket above and below the calliper. Those ears that make is so the calliper has to be straight or you can't slide it into position.))<P>Many people think the slide bolts take the braking load, but they don't. The main force of stopping is transmitted directly to the support bracket.<P>At rest the caliper doesn't contact the bracket because of the rubber bushings in the slide holes. Those bushings don't just keep out dirt. they actually help the caliper to properly float in the bracket so it doesn't drag a brake pad.<P>If dirt or rust build up in the slack space between the caliper and the reaction stop ears on the bracket you can also get creaky brakes. When you took the brakes apart to change pads you likely also knocked a fair amount of crap out of the slack space.<P>Next time you have the wheels off for any reason you can lube the reaction ears with a little antisieze and a little shim or something to work it into the gap. you don't need to disassemble things unless they are jammed up. Good preventive thingy.....||||reg||7
Z||000002||Mark||10-12-1999||11:23 PM||||I once had a problem with rattling rear brake pads on my '87 6000 STE. These brakes also use the stainless steel ring thingie to retain the pads to the caliper pistons. I bent the tabs very slightly on the stainless steel ring to increase the holding force on the pads.  Instant silence!  ||||reg||1
